        chapters = listOf(
            EnChapter(1, "The Crime", listOf(
                "On an exceptionally hot evening in early July, a young man named Raskolnikov left his tiny attic room in the slums of St. Petersburg and walked toward the apartment of an old pawnbroker named Alyona Ivanovna.",
                "He had been planning this visit for weeks, not as a customer, but as a murderer, and he carried with him an axe that he had hidden inside his coat.",
                "He was a former student, a young man of great intelligence and greater poverty, and he had convinced himself that the world would be better if the old woman were dead.",
                "She was a mean and cruel creature, he told himself, who treated her sister like a slave and who hoarded wealth that could save hundreds of lives.",
                "He had written an article, which he had published in a newspaper, in which he argued that extraordinary men had the right to commit crimes if those crimes served a higher purpose.",
                "He believed himself to be such a man, and so, on that hot July evening, he climbed the stairs to her door and rang the bell.",
                "She opened the door, suspicious as always, and he walked in, pretending to have a watch that he wished to pawn.",
                "As she turned to fetch some money, he raised the axe and struck her on the head, killing her almost instantly.",
                "And then, in a moment of horror, he discovered that her sister Lizaveta had returned and was standing in the doorway, staring at him with wide and terrified eyes.",
                "He killed her too, not because he wanted to, but because he had no choice, because she had seen him, because she would tell the police.",
                "He grabbed what valuables he could find and fled the apartment, but he was in such a state of shock that he barely knew what he was doing.",
                "He almost forgot to take the money, and when he remembered, he took only a few coins, and then he wandered through the streets of the city, unable to believe what he had done.",
                "He returned to his room and fell asleep, and when he woke, he discovered that he had not properly closed the door, that anyone could have walked in and discovered him.",
                "Days passed, and the police began their investigation, and Raskolnikov grew feverish, and his mind began to unravel.",
                "He received a summons to the police station, and he thought he was being arrested, but it turned out to be only a matter of an unpaid debt.",
                "He fainted in the station, and when he woke, he heard the police discussing the murder, and one of them mentioned that the killer had almost certainly been among the pawnbroker's customers.",
                "Raskolnikov returned home more terrified than ever, and he began to see the whole world as a threat, as a conspiracy against him.",
                "His mother and sister arrived in the city, summoned by letters he had written in his madness, and he was overjoyed to see them, and also terrified, for he had brought them into the middle of his nightmare.",
                "He met a man named Razumikhin, a former friend and fellow student, who was cheerful and kind and completely unaware of what Raskolnikov had done.",
                "And he met a young woman named Sonya, a prostitute, the daughter of a drunkard, whose family was starving and who had sacrificed herself to save them.",
                "She was, in the midst of all that filth and suffering, a kind of saint, and Raskolnikov, who had murdered for what he thought was a great purpose, was drawn to her like a man in the desert is drawn to water.",
                "But he did not yet confess, and the confession, when it came, would be the beginning of the punishment.",
                "For Raskolnikov had discovered, in the weeks that followed the murders, that his great theory had been a lie, that he was not an extraordinary man, but a weak and trembling creature like everyone else.",
                "He had wanted to prove that he had the right to kill, and he had proved instead that he had neither the right nor the strength, and the discovery was destroying him.",
                "And so he continued to wander through the streets of St. Petersburg, a man without a purpose, a man without a God, a man who had committed the greatest of sins and who now had to live with the consequences.",
                "For he had believed that the murder would free him, and it had enslaved him instead, and he had believed that he would become a Napoleon, and he had become something less than a man.",
                "He had believed that he could endure the crime, and he had discovered that he could not, and now he was trapped in a hell of his own making, with no way out and no way back.",
                "The days passed, and the police drew closer, and Raskolnikov grew more and more certain that he would be found out.",
                "He was summoned again to the police station, and this time he was confronted by a man named Porfiry Petrovich, a magistrate who had read Raskolnikov's article and who suspected him of the murders.",
                "Porfiry did not arrest him, but he began to play a game with him, asking questions, hinting at things, waiting for Raskolnikov to betray himself.",
                "And Raskolnikov, in his feverish state, began to imagine that he saw the dead women in the streets, and to hear the sounds of the murder in his ears, and to feel the terror of the deed pressing down on him every waking hour.",
                "And he met a man named Svidrigailov, a man who had been in love with his sister Dunya, a man who was a murderer himself, and who recognized in Raskolnikov a kindred spirit.",
                "Svidrigailov told him that he too had done terrible things, and that he too had found no peace, and that in the end the only thing left to do was to accept the punishment and to live with it.",
                "And Raskolnikov heard him, and understood him, and did not yet follow his example, for he was still trying to escape, still trying to believe that he could survive without confession, without punishment, without atonement.",
                "But the more he tried to escape, the more he realized that there was no escape, and that the only way out was through the deepest, most terrible, most necessary of confessions.",
                "For he had killed two women, and he had destroyed his own soul, and he had learned that even the greatest of theories cannot justify the smallest of murders.",
                "And so the first chapter of Crime and Punishment ends, and Raskolnikov is still free, still alive, still tormented, and the real punishment has only just begun."
            )),
            EnChapter(2, "The Confession", listOf(
                "Sonya was the only one who truly loved him, and Raskolnikov knew it, and that was why he went to her, and why he told her what he had done.",
                "It was late at night, and he had come to her room, and he had spoken to her of the murders as though he were speaking of someone else, and she had listened without understanding, until suddenly she did understand.",
                "She understood that he was the one who had killed the old pawnbroker and her sister, and she understood that he had come to her because she was the only person in the world who could help him.",
                "She wept, and she knelt before him, and she told him that he had destroyed himself, that there was no human being more miserable in the world than he, and that she would never abandon him, no matter what he had done.",
                "And then she told him that he must confess, that he must go to the police station and kneel before the whole world, and that he must accept the punishment that would follow.",
                "For the punishment, she said, was not the sentence of the court, but the suffering that would restore his soul, and that would unite him with all the other suffering human beings in the world.",
                "Raskolnikov listened, and he understood, and he did not go to the police station that night, and he did not go the next night, and he did not go for many nights, for he was still afraid.",
                "But he was no longer the same man who had committed the murders, for he had begun to understand that the murder had not made him an extraordinary man, but a common criminal, and that the only way to become extraordinary again was to confess.",
                "He saw his mother, and he saw his sister, and he saw the pain that he had caused them, and he understood that the punishment he was about to accept was as much for them as for himself.",
                "And he saw Sonya, who had sacrificed everything to save her family, and who loved him despite his crimes, and he understood that such a love was worth more than all the theories in the world.",
                "And so, one evening, he went to the police station, and he stood before the chief of police, and he tried to speak, and the words would not come, and the chief of police told him that he looked ill, and that he should go home and rest.",
                "But Raskolnikov did not go home, and instead he went out into the street, and there he saw Sonya, standing in the square, waiting for him, and he knew that he could not go on without her.",
                "He went back into the police station, and this time, he spoke, and the words that he spoke were these: I killed the old pawnbroker and her sister with an axe.",
                "The chief of police could not believe what he had heard, and he asked Raskolnikov to repeat himself, and Raskolnikov repeated himself, and then the whole world seemed to turn upside down.",
                "The police came, and they took him away, and he did not resist, and as he was being led out of the station, he saw Sonya, and she was weeping, and she was also smiling, and he knew that he would not be alone.",
                "The trial lasted many weeks, and Raskolnikov did not try to defend himself, and he did not try to excuse his crime, and he simply told the truth, and the truth was enough to condemn him.",
                "But the judges, in their mercy, took into account his confession, and his youth, and the terrible poverty in which he had lived, and they sentenced him to eight years of hard labor in Siberia.",
                "Sonya did not abandon him, and she followed him to Siberia, and she worked as a seamstress and a nurse, and she visited him in the prison, and she sent him books and letters and medicines.",
                "And Raskolnikov, in the cold and the darkness and the endless labor of the prison, began, at last, to understand what he had done, and to feel the beginning of a remorse that was also a kind of love.",
                "For he had loved no one, and he had believed in nothing, and he had thought that he could stand above the world, and in the end the world had crushed him, and it had been right to crush him.",
                "But Sonya did not let the world crush him completely, and she held onto him with both hands, and she would not let him go.",
                "And he began to read the books she gave him, and he began to think about what they said, and he began to feel a strange new feeling, a feeling that he did not recognize, and that he would later learn to call love.",
                "And when he finally fell at her feet, in the courtyard of the prison, in the gray light of dawn, and wept, and kissed her hands, she understood that he loved her, and that they would be together, and that the suffering would be worth it.",
                "For this was the beginning of the punishment that was also the beginning of the redemption, and the punishment was the love that he had been seeking all his life.",
                "And so the second chapter of Crime and Punishment ends, and Raskolnikov has confessed, and Sonya has followed him to Siberia, and the story has found its beginning.",
                "But this was not the end, was far from the end, and would never truly be the end, for the punishment of a crime is not a sentence that ends, but a suffering that transforms.",
                "And Raskolnikov, in the years of his imprisonment, would come to understand this, and would come, at last, to accept it, and in the accepting, would find a strange peace.",
                "For the peace was not the peace of innocence, but the peace of a man who knows what he has done, and who bears the weight of it, and who does not try to put it down.",
                "And this peace, this terrible peace, this peace that no one else could understand, was the only peace that Raskolnikov would ever know.",
                "He would write to his mother and sister, and he would tell them that he was alive, and that they should not worry, and that he was well, and that he loved them.",
                "And they would write back, and they would send him parcels and letters, and they would pray for him, and they would hope for him, and they would never quite understand what had happened to him.",
                "But Sonya understood, and Sonya loved him, and Sonya stayed with him, and that was enough, that was more than enough, that was everything.",
                "And so the story of Crime and Punishment comes to an end, not with a triumph, not with a salvation, not with a resolution, but with a love that is stronger than sin, and a suffering that is also a kind of peace.",
                "For in the end, Raskolnikov learned that the most terrible thing in the world is not death, but life without meaning, and that the only thing that can give life meaning is love, and that the only way to find love is to accept the punishment that love requires."
            )),
            EnChapter(3, "The Investigation", listOf(
                "After the murders, Raskolnikov was summoned to the police station, not because he was a suspect, but because his landlord had complained about an unpaid debt.",
                "And it was there, standing before the chief of police, that he first heard about the murders, and he learned that the police had no suspects, and that they were entirely in the dark.",
                "He fainted on the spot, and when he recovered, he was even more terrified than before, for he now knew that the police did not suspect him, and yet he was sure that they would eventually discover the truth.",
                "Days passed, and he received a letter from his mother, telling him that his sister Dunya was about to marry a man named Luzhin, a man who was rich and respectable, but who was also cruel and arrogant.",
                "And Raskolnikov, who had loved his sister more than anyone in the world, was determined to stop the marriage, and he was even more determined when he learned the reason for it.",
                "For his sister was marrying Luzhin not out of love but out of duty, out of a desire to help her mother and her brother, out of the kind of self-sacrifice that Raskolnikov both admired and could not bear.",
                "He went to the meeting that had been arranged between his mother, his sister, and Luzhin, and he confronted the man, and he told him that he would never allow the marriage.",
                "And Luzhin, who was a small and petty man, was furious, and he left the meeting, and he swore that he would have his revenge.",
                "And Raskolnikov, in his feverish state, began to suspect that Luzhin was the murderer, and he began to follow him through the streets, and he began to imagine that everyone was plotting against him.",
                "It was at this time that he met Porfiry Petrovich, a magistrate who had read his article about extraordinary men and who wanted to discuss it with him.",
                "Porfiry was a strange man, a man who laughed at the wrong moments, and who seemed to know more than he said, and who seemed to enjoy playing with his prey.",
                "And Raskolnikov, who was not a fool, understood at once that Porfiry suspected him, and he understood that Porfiry was not going to arrest him, not yet, but was going to torture him with questions and hints and innuendos.",
                "And Porfiry did, and the interview lasted for hours, and Raskolnikov left it in a state of complete collapse, more terrified than ever, and more certain than ever that he had been found out.",
                "But Porfiry did not arrest him, and Raskolnikov began to wonder whether the whole thing was a game, or whether Porfiry was simply waiting for him to make a mistake.",
                "And Raskolnikov made a mistake, and he made it in the most public way possible, for he began to talk about the murders, and he began to defend the murderer, and he began to say that the killer had done nothing wrong.",
                "And a workman who had been present at the scene of the crime recognized him, and he followed him through the streets, and he called him a murderer, and the whole world seemed to close in around him.",
                "Raskolnikov went home and fell into a fever, and he lay in his bed for days, and he dreamed of the murders, and he dreamed of the old woman laughing at him, and he dreamed that he could not kill her, no matter how many times he tried.",
                "When he woke, he found that his mother and his sister had arrived in the city, and they were staying with Razumikhin, and they were waiting for him.",
                "And Raskolnikov, who had thought he was alone in the world, discovered that he was not, and that the people he loved were worried about him, and that they would do anything to help him.",
                "And yet he could not tell them the truth, for the truth would destroy them, and so he continued to hide, and continued to suffer, and continued to wait for the moment when the world would finally find him out.",
                "It was during this time that Raskolnikov met Sonya, and it was during this time that he realized that she was the only person in the world who could help him.",
                "For Sonya had also been destroyed by the world, and Sonya had also sacrificed herself for her family, and Sonya had also learned that the only way to survive was to accept the suffering that life brings.",
                "And Raskolnikov, in his madness, began to think that Sonya was his only hope, and he began to think that if he could confess to Sonya, then everything would be all right.",
                "And so, one evening, he went to Sonya's room, and he told her what he had done, and he watched as her face changed from confusion to horror to understanding.",
                "And Sonya did not run away, and Sonya did not condemn him, and Sonya simply wept, and held him, and told him that she would never leave him.",
                "And that, for Raskolnikov, was the beginning of his salvation, and the beginning of his punishment, for he now knew that he was not alone, and that he would have to face the consequences of his crime.",
                "For the loneliness had been his greatest comfort, and the love was now his greatest burden, and the burden would not let him rest until he had done what he knew he must do.",
                "The days passed, and the police investigation continued, and Porfiry continued to play his strange and terrible game, and Raskolnikov continued to sink deeper into his fever.",
                "And then, one day, Porfiry told him the truth, and the truth was that he had no evidence, and that he could not arrest Raskolnikov, and that the only thing that could convict him was his own confession.",
                "And Porfiry told him to confess, and he told him to confess not for the sake of the police, but for the sake of his own soul, and for the sake of his own peace.",
                "And Raskolnikov listened, and he understood, and he did not confess that day, but he knew that he would, and he knew that it would be soon.",
                "For the confession was not the end of the punishment, but its beginning, and the punishment was the only way to peace, and the peace was the only thing that mattered."
            )),
            EnChapter(4, "Svidrigailov", listOf(
                "There was a man in St. Petersburg named Svidrigailov, and he was one of the most dangerous and most fascinating figures in the novel, and he was also, in his own way, a mirror image of Raskolnikov.",
                "He had been a landowner, and he had been a gambler, and he had been a murderer, and he had also been in love with Raskolnikov's sister, and he had driven her nearly to madness with his attentions.",
                "And when he arrived in St. Petersburg, he did not come to help Raskolnikov, but to pursue his sister, and to do so in the most subtle and most dangerous way possible.",
                "He was charming, and he was intelligent, and he was utterly without moral scruple, and he knew that he was all of these things, and he did not apologize for any of them.",
                "And he recognized in Raskolnikov a kindred spirit, a man who had also crossed the line that separates the ordinary world from the world of those who have committed murder.",
                "And he spoke to Raskolnikov, and he told him that he understood what he had done, and that he did not judge him for it, and that in the end the only thing left to do was to accept the punishment that the world would impose.",
                "And Svidrigailov told Raskolnikov that he had learned this lesson from his own life, and that he had learned it the hard way, and that he would not wish such a lesson on his worst enemy.",
                "He told him that he had done terrible things, and that he had lived with them, and that the living was the hardest part.",
                "For the murder is one thing, and the living afterward is another thing entirely, and it is the living that destroys a man, not the murder.",
                "And Raskolnikov heard him, and understood him, and did not want to understand him, for to understand him would be to admit that he had become what Svidrigailov was.",
                "But he had become what Svidrigailov was, and the more he tried to deny it, the more true it became, and the more terrible the truth became.",
                "And Svidrigailov continued to pursue Dunya, and to pursue her in a way that Raskolnikov could not stop, and Raskolnikov watched helplessly as his sister was drawn into the trap that Svidrigailov had set for her.",
                "But Dunya was stronger than Svidrigailov had expected, and when he tried to force her to submit to him, she drew a revolver and fired at him, and the bullet grazed his temple, and he stood there with blood running down his face and did not move.",
                "For Svidrigailov had realized, in that moment, that he could not have what he wanted, and that the only way to end his suffering was to end his life, and so he decided to do so that very night.",
                "He went to a poor hotel, and he gave away his money to a young girl who had been abused by her family, and he walked out into the rain, and he shot himself in the head, and he died.",
                "And his death, and his last act of kindness, and his suicide, and his acceptance of the punishment that he had earned, all of these things were a lesson to Raskolnikov.",
                "For Svidrigailov had shown him what he could become if he did not confess, and Svidrigailov had shown him that there was no escape from the crime except through the punishment.",
                "And Raskolnikov, who had been running from the punishment for weeks, who had been trying to escape it, who had been hoping against hope that he could avoid it, finally understood that he could not.",
                "He went to Sonya, and he wept, and he asked her to give him the cross that she wore around her neck, and she did, and he put it on, and he felt, for the first time in weeks, a strange kind of peace.",
                "And Sonya said the words that she had been waiting to say since the moment he had confessed to her, and the words were these: go to the crossroads, bow down to the whole world, and say aloud, I am a murderer.",
                "And Raskolnikov went to the crossroads, and he tried to say the words, and the words would not come, and he broke down and wept, and he did not say them, and yet he had begun the journey that would lead him to the saying.",
                "And when he finally did say them, in the police station, in front of the chief of police, he knew that he had accepted the punishment, and that the punishment was the beginning of his redemption.",
                "And the days that followed, the days of the trial, and the days of the sentencing, and the days of the long journey to Siberia, all of these days were the working out of the redemption that had begun at the crossroads.",
                "And Svidrigailov, who had killed himself, and who had given away his money, and who had accepted the punishment that the world could not give him, was a part of that redemption too, for his life had been a warning, and his death had been a lesson.",
                "And so the fourth chapter of Crime and Punishment ends, and Svidrigailov has died, and Raskolnikov has confessed, and the story has reached its turning point, and the punishment has begun."
            )),
            EnChapter(5, "The Punishment", listOf(
                "The trial was long and painful, not only for Raskolnikov but also for his mother and his sister and Sonya, who all attended the hearings and who all suffered with him.",
                "The evidence against him was overwhelming, and his confession was complete, and yet the judges, in their strange mercy, decided to spare his life.",
                "They took into account his youth, his poverty, his sincere confession, and the fact that he had committed the murders in a state of temporary insanity, and they sentenced him to eight years of hard labor in Siberia.",
                "And Raskolnikov heard the sentence, and he felt nothing, for he had already accepted the punishment in his heart, and the punishment that the court could give him was nothing compared to the punishment that he was giving himself.",
                "He was taken to Siberia, and Sonya followed him, and she took a small room near the prison, and she worked as a seamstress, and she visited him whenever the rules allowed.",
                "And Raskolnikov, in the prison, was at first silent, and withdrawn, and hostile to everyone, and he refused to speak to Sonya, and he refused to see her, and he refused to accept her love.",
                "For he still believed, at some level, that he was an extraordinary man, and that he had only failed because he had been weak, and that if he had been stronger he would have been able to bear the crime.",
                "But the months passed, and the isolation wore on him, and he began, slowly, to understand that his theory had been nonsense, and that the only thing that had made him different from other men was that he had killed.",
                "And he began to see that the suffering of the prison was not the punishment, but the cure, and that the cure was slow and painful, and that it required him to give up the pride that had led him to murder.",
                "And Sonya did not give up on him, and she came to him, week after week, and she sat with him, and she said nothing, and she simply was there.",
                "And the other prisoners did not understand her, and they called her a saint, and they called her a fool, and they loved her, and she loved them, and she loved Raskolnikov, and she did not stop loving him.",
                "And one day, in the courtyard of the prison, in the gray light of a Siberian dawn, Raskolnikov saw Sonya standing at the gate, and he saw her face, and he saw her love, and something inside him broke.",
                "He went to her, and he fell at her feet, and he wept, and he kissed her hands, and he did not say anything, and he did not need to say anything.",
                "For the pride had finally broken, and in its place there was something else, something that he had never felt before, something that he would later learn to call love.",
                "And Sonya understood, and she wept too, and she held him, and she knew that he had finally accepted the punishment, and that the acceptance was the beginning of the redemption.",
                "For the punishment, as she had always known, was not the eight years of hard labor, but the slow surrender of the pride that had caused the crime, and the slow discovery of the love that could heal it.",
                "And Raskolnikov did not become a saint, and he did not become a hero, and he did not become anything more than a man, but he became, at last, a man who knew what he had done, and who would spend the rest of his life trying to be worthy of the love that had saved him.",
                "And he read the books that Sonya gave him, and he thought about the God that she believed in, and he did not yet believe, but he began to wonder whether there was something in faith after all.",
                "And the years passed, and the eight years of punishment became seven, and then six, and then five, and the day of freedom approached, and Raskolnikov began to plan his life with Sonya.",
                "For she had waited for him, and she had loved him, and she had shown him that there was, after all, a way to live with what he had done.",
                "And the way was not the way of the extraordinary man, the way of the murderer, the way of the man who believed that he was above the world, but the way of the ordinary man, the way of the humble man, the way of the man who accepts his guilt and who lives with it.",
                "And this way, this slow and painful and quiet way, was the only way that led to peace, and Raskolnikov had finally learned this, and he would carry the lesson with him for the rest of his life.",
                "And when he was finally released, he went to Sonya, and he took her hand, and he said the only words that mattered, and the words were these: I love you.",
                "And Sonya wept, and she said the same words back, and they walked out of the prison together, into the cold Siberian sun, and they knew that they were free.",
                "Not free from suffering, not free from the past, not free from the memory of what Raskolnikov had done, but free from the loneliness that had made him a murderer, and free from the pride that had made him a monster.",
                "And so the story of Crime and Punishment comes to an end, not with a triumph, not with a salvation, not with a resolution, but with a love that is stronger than sin, and a suffering that is also a kind of peace.",
                "For the punishment was the love, and the love was the punishment, and the only way to accept one was to accept the other, and the only way to accept both was to surrender the pride that had made them both necessary.",
                "And Raskolnikov, in the years that followed, would continue to struggle, and would continue to suffer, and would continue to wonder whether he had been forgiven or only forgiven himself.",
                "But the struggle was the point, and the suffering was the point, and the wondering was the point, and all of it together was the punishment that had become the redemption.",
                "For in the end, the only thing that can save a man who has committed murder is the confession of his guilt, and the acceptance of his punishment, and the love of another human being who knows the worst about him and loves him anyway.",
                "And Raskolnikov had found such a love, and had accepted such a punishment, and had made such a confession, and so he was saved.",
                "Not saved from the world, not saved from himself, not saved from the memory of what he had done, but saved from the loneliness that had made him a murderer, and saved from the pride that had made him a monster.",
                "And so the fifth and final chapter of Crime and Punishment comes to its close, and the story is complete, and the redemption has begun."
            ))
        )
    )

    private fun story2() = EnStory(
        storyId = "adv_the_metamorphosis",
        chapters = listOf(
            EnChapter(1, "The Transformation", listOf(
                "One morning, when Gregor Samsa woke from troubled dreams, he found himself transformed in his bed into a horrible vermin, and he lay on his armor-like back, and he saw his brown belly, slightly domed and divided by arches into stiff sections.",
                "He was lying on his back, and his many legs, pitifully thin compared with the size of the rest of him, waved helplessly before his eyes.",
                "What has happened to me, he thought, and it was not a dream, for his room, a proper human room although a little too small, lay peacefully between its four familiar walls.",
                "A collection of textile samples lay spread out on the table, and above it there hung a picture that he had recently cut out of an illustrated magazine and housed in a nice gilded frame.",
                "Gregor was a traveling salesman, a man who had worked for the same firm for five years, a man who had never missed a day of work, a man who had never been late.",
                "He was the sole provider for his family, for his mother, his father, and his sister Grete, and he had worked himself to the bone to pay off his father's debts.",
                "And now, on this ordinary morning, he could not get out of bed, for he did not know how to use his new body, and he did not know what had happened to him, and he did not know what to do.",
                "He tried to move, and he found that he could move, but not in the way he was used to, and not in the way he needed to, and the discovery filled him with terror.",
                "He tried to call for help, and the sound that came out of him was not his voice, but a strange and inhuman cry, and he fell silent, and he wept.",
                "His mother called to him from the other room, and asked him if he was all right, and told him that he was late for his train, and that he should hurry.",
                "His father called to him, and told him to get up, and told him that the chief clerk from the office had come to ask about him, and that he should come out and explain himself.",
                "And Gregor, in his room, tried to speak, and tried to open the door, and tried to become himself again, and he could not do any of these things.",
                "The chief clerk shouted through the door, and told Gregor that he was lazy, and that he was irresponsible, and that he would be dismissed if he did not come out at once.",
